/* xCenbar Learning — books from the learner's own documents: Reading Book + Verb lesson */
window.XC=window.XC||{};
XC.bookReading={"texts": [{"n": 1, "t": "My Country, Cambodia", "lv": "A2", "sec": "A", "body": "Cambodia is a country in Southeast Asia. It is next to Thailand, Laos and Vietnam. About seventeen million people live here. The capital city is Phnom Penh. Most people are Khmer, and they speak the Khmer language. Cambodia is warm all year. There are two main seasons: the rainy season, from May to October, and the dry season, from November to April. The country is not very big, but it has beautiful rivers, mountains, beaches and old temples. Many tourists visit Cambodia every year. They like the food, the culture and the kind people.", "words": [["capital", "រាជធានី"], ["season", "រដូវ"], ["rainy", "ភ្លៀង"], ["tourist", "ភ្ញៀវទេសចរ"], ["culture", "វប្បធម៌"], ["kind", "ចិត្តល្អ"]], "q": ["What is the capital city of Cambodia?", "Which months are the rainy season?"], "a": ["Phnom Penh.", "From May to October."]}, {"n": 2, "t": "Angkor Wat", "lv": "A2", "sec": "A", "body": "Angkor Wat is the most famous temple in Cambodia. It is near the city of Siem Reap. King Suryavarman II built it in the early twelfth century. At first, it was a Hindu temple, but later it became a Buddhist temple. Angkor Wat is very big, and it has five towers shaped like lotus buds. It is also on the Cambodian flag. Many visitors go there early in the morning to watch the sunrise behind the towers. If you go, bring water and a hat, because it is very hot.", "words": [["temple", "ប្រាសាទ"], ["tower", "ប៉ម"], ["lotus", "ឈូក"], ["flag", "ទង់ជាតិ"], ["sunrise", "ថ្ងៃរះ"], ["visitor", "អ្នកទស្សនា"]], "q": ["Who built Angkor Wat?", "Why should visitors bring water and a hat?"], "a": ["King Suryavarman II.", "Because it is very hot."]}, {"n": 3, "t": "The Tonlé Sap Lake", "lv": "B1", "sec": "A", "body": "The Tonlé Sap is the biggest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia, and it is special for one amazing reason: its river changes direction. During the dry season, water flows from the lake into the Mekong River through the Tonlé Sap River. But when the rainy season begins and the Mekong becomes very full, the river flows backwards, and water enters the lake. As a result, the lake grows to several times its normal size. Millions of Cambodians depend on it, because it provides fish, which are an important source of protein. Many families live in floating villages and move their houses as the water rises and falls.", "words": [["freshwater", "ទឹកសាប"], ["direction", "ទិសដៅ"], ["backwards", "ថយក្រោយ"], ["depend on", "ពឹងផ្អែកលើ"], ["protein", "ប្រូតេអ៊ីន"], ["floating village", "ភូមិអណ្តែតទឹក"]], "q": ["What happens to the river in the rainy season?", "Why is the lake important for Cambodians?"], "a": ["It flows backwards, and water enters the lake.", "It provides fish, an important source of protein."]}, {"n": 4, "t": "Cambodian Food", "lv": "A2", "sec": "A", "body": "Cambodian food is fresh, simple and delicious. Rice is the most important food, and most meals are eaten with rice. A famous dish is fish amok, which is fish cooked with coconut milk and spices, usually steamed in a banana leaf. Another favourite is lok lak, which is beef served with a sauce made from lime and black pepper. For breakfast, many people eat noodle soup or rice porridge. Cambodian food is not always very spicy, but people often add chilli, lime or fish sauce at the table. Kampot pepper, from the south of the country, is famous around the world.", "words": [["dish", "មុខម្ហូប"], ["steamed", "ចំហុយ"], ["spice", "គ្រឿងទេស"], ["porridge", "បបរ"], ["favourite", "ចូលចិត្តបំផុត"], ["sauce", "ទឹកជ្រលក់"]], "q": ["What is fish amok cooked with?", "What do many people eat for breakfast?"], "a": ["Coconut milk and spices.", "Noodle soup or rice porridge."]}, {"n": 5, "t": "Khmer New Year", "lv": "B1", "sec": "A", "body": "Khmer New Year, called Chaul Chnam Thmey, is the biggest holiday in Cambodia. It takes place in mid-April, after the harvest and before the rains begin. The celebration lasts three days. People clean their houses, visit temples, give food to monks and ask older family members for blessings. Young people play traditional games, such as tug of war and leak kanseng, where players hide a scarf behind someone. In the afternoon, friends and family throw water and powder at each other for fun. Many people who work in the city travel back to their home villages, so the roads are very busy.", "words": [["harvest", "ការច្រូតកាត់"], ["monk", "ព្រះសង្ឃ"], ["blessing", "ពរជ័យ"], ["traditional", "ប្រពៃណី "], ["scarf", "កន្សែង"], ["celebration", "ការប្រារព្ធពិធី"]], "q": ["How many days does the celebration last?", "Why are the roads busy during Khmer New Year?"], "a": ["Three days.", "Because many people travel back to their home villages."]}, {"n": 6, "t": "Phnom Penh", "lv": "A2", "sec": "A", "body": "Phnom Penh is the capital and the biggest city in Cambodia. It is where three rivers meet: the Mekong, the Tonlé Sap and the Bassac. The city is busy and colourful. There are many motorbikes, tuk-tuks and small shops on the street. You can visit the Royal Palace, the National Museum and the Central Market, which has a big yellow dome. In the evening, many people walk along the riverside, exercise in the park or eat street food with friends. Phnom Penh is a mix of old and modern life.", "words": [["meet", "ជួប"], ["palace", "ព្រះបរមរាជវាំង"], ["museum", "សារមន្ទីរ"], ["dome", "ដំបូលរាងមូល"], ["riverside", "មាត់ទន្លេ"], ["modern", "ទំនើប"]], "q": ["Which three rivers meet in Phnom Penh?", "What do people do in the evening?"], "a": ["The Mekong, the Tonlé Sap and the Bassac.", "They walk by the river, exercise in the park or eat street food with friends."]}, {"n": 7, "t": "The Water Festival", "lv": "B2", "sec": "A", "body": "Every November, Cambodia celebrates Bon Om Touk, known as the Water Festival. It marks the end of the rainy season and the moment when the Tonlé Sap River starts flowing back towards the Mekong. In Phnom Penh, hundreds of long wooden boats, each carrying dozens of rowers, race along the river, while huge crowds cheer from the banks. In the evenings, boats decorated with lights sail past, and there is music, food and fireworks. Beyond the entertainment, the festival is a way of thanking the river, which supports fishing and farming across the country. For many families, it is also a rare chance to gather together in the capital and share the excitement.", "words": [["festival", "ពិធីបុណ្យ"], ["rower", "អ្នកចែវទូក"], ["cheer", "ស្រែកលើកទឹកចិត្ត"], ["decorated", "តុបតែង"], ["entertainment", "ការកម្សាន្ត"], ["gather", "ជួបជុំ"]], "q": ["What does the Water Festival mark?", "Why do Cambodians thank the river?"], "a": ["The end of the rainy season, when the Tonlé Sap River flows back towards the Mekong.", "Because it supports fishing and farming across the country."]}, {"n": 8, "t": "Khmer Arts", "lv": "B2", "sec": "A", "body": "Cambodia has an old and rich artistic tradition. Apsara dance, with its slow hand movements, graceful poses and elegant costumes, represents the heavenly dancers carved on the walls of the Angkor temples. During the difficult years of the 1970s, many artists lost their lives, and the dance almost disappeared. However, the few survivors taught it to a new generation, and today it is performed proudly for visitors and at national ceremonies. Another tradition is bokator, an ancient martial art that was also nearly lost and is now taught again in schools and clubs. Together, these arts show how Cambodians keep their culture alive despite hardship.", "words": [["graceful", "ទន់ភ្លន់"], ["costume", "សំលៀកបំពាក់"], ["carved", "ឆ្លាក់"], ["survivor", "អ្នករួចជីវិត"], ["ancient", "បុរាណ"], ["martial art", "សិល្បៈក្បាច់គុន"], ["hardship", "ការលំបាក"]], "q": ["What does Apsara dance represent?", "How was the dance saved?"], "a": ["The heavenly dancers carved on the walls of the Angkor temples.", "The few survivors taught it to a new generation."]}, {"n": 9, "t": "A Morning in Phnom Penh", "lv": "A2", "sec": "B", "body": "My name is Sophea, and I live in Phnom Penh. I wake up at six o'clock every morning. First, I wash my face and brush my teeth. Then I walk to a small shop near my house. I usually buy bai sach chrouk, which is grilled pork with rice, and an iced coffee with sweet milk. The street is already busy. Motorbikes go past, a woman sells fruit, and children walk to school with their parents. At seven thirty, I take my bag and go to work by motorbike. I like mornings because the air is cooler and the city feels fresh.", "words": [["wake up", "ភ្ញាក់ពីដំណេក"], ["grilled", "អាំង"], ["iced coffee", "កាហ្វេទឹកកក"], ["already", "រួចទៅហើយ"], ["cooler", "ត្រជាក់ជាង"]], "q": ["What does Sophea usually buy for breakfast?", "Why does she like mornings?"], "a": ["Bai sach chrouk and an iced coffee with sweet milk.", "Because the air is cooler and the city feels fresh."]}, {"n": 10, "t": "At the Market", "lv": "A2", "sec": "B", "body": "Most Cambodian families buy their food at a local market. Early in the morning, the market is full of people. Sellers put out fresh vegetables, fish, meat, fruit and herbs on the tables. The smell of fresh bread and grilled fish is everywhere. Prices are not always fixed, so buyers can ask for a lower price. You can say, \"How much is this?\" and then, \"Can you make it cheaper?\" Many sellers accept both riel and US dollars. After shopping, people often stop for a cup of tea or a bowl of noodles before going home.", "words": [["vegetable", "បន្លែ"], ["herb", "ស្មៅម្ហូប"], ["fixed", "ថេរ"], ["cheaper", "ថោកជាង"], ["accept", "ទទួល"], ["riel", "រៀល"]], "q": ["What can buyers ask for in a market?", "Which two currencies do many sellers accept?"], "a": ["A lower price.", "Riel and US dollars."]}, {"n": 11, "t": "Going to School", "lv": "A2", "sec": "B", "body": "In Cambodia, many students wear a uniform to school. Most girls and boys wear a white shirt with dark trousers or a dark skirt. Some schools start in the morning, and others start in the afternoon, so students can share the same building. They study Khmer, mathematics, science, history and English. After school, some students go to extra classes to practise English or computers. Others help their parents at home or in the family shop. Education is very important for many families, because they believe a good education gives children a better future.", "words": [["uniform", "ឯកសណ្ឋាន"], ["trousers", "ខោ"], ["skirt", "សំពត់"], ["extra", "បន្ថែម"], ["education", "ការអប់រំ"], ["future", "អនាគត"]], "q": ["What do most students wear to school?", "Why is education important for many families?"], "a": ["A uniform: a white shirt with dark trousers or a dark skirt.", "They believe it gives children a better future."]}, {"n": 12, "t": "Family Dinner", "lv": "B1", "sec": "B", "body": "In the evening, many Cambodian families eat dinner together. Instead of giving each person a separate plate of food, the family puts several dishes in the middle of the table, usually rice, a soup, a vegetable dish and some fish or meat. Everyone takes food from the shared dishes and puts it on their own bowl of rice. Children are taught to show respect by waiting for the older people to start eating first. During the meal, family members talk about their day, discuss problems and make plans for the weekend. Even in busy families, this time is considered important because it helps everyone feel close to each other.", "words": [["separate", "ដាច់ដោយឡែក"], ["shared", "ចែករំលែក"], ["respect", "ការគោរព"], ["discuss", "ពិភាក្សា"], ["considered", "ត្រូវបានចាត់ទុក"], ["close", "ជិតស្និទ្ធ"]], "q": ["How is the food served at a family dinner?", "Why is dinner time important for many families?"], "a": ["Several dishes are put in the middle and everyone shares them.", "It helps everyone feel close to each other."]}, {"n": 13, "t": "Getting Around", "lv": "B1", "sec": "B", "body": "People in Cambodian cities use many kinds of transport. The most popular vehicle is the motorbike, because it is cheap and can move easily through heavy traffic. For short trips, many people take a tuk-tuk, which is a motorbike pulling a small carriage with seats. These days, you can also order a ride by using a phone app, which is convenient for tourists who do not speak Khmer. Everyone on a motorbike is supposed to wear a helmet, as it protects the head in an accident. Although traffic can seem crazy at first, most drivers move slowly and carefully, so you quickly get used to it.", "words": [["transport", "ការដឹកជញ្ជូន"], ["traffic", "ចរាចរណ៍ "], ["carriage", "រទេះ"], ["convenient", "ងាយស្រួល"], ["helmet", "មួកសុវត្ថិភាព"], ["get used to", "សម្របខ្លួន"]], "q": ["Why is the motorbike so popular?", "Why should you wear a helmet?"], "a": ["It is cheap and moves easily through heavy traffic.", "It protects the head in an accident."]}, {"n": 14, "t": "Working Life", "lv": "B1", "sec": "B", "body": "Cambodians do many different kinds of jobs. In the countryside, a large number of people are farmers who grow rice, vegetables and fruit. In cities, many young people work in factories that make clothes and shoes for foreign brands, while others work in hotels, restaurants and tour companies because tourism is a major industry. Some people start small businesses, such as a noodle shop, a hair salon or an online store on social media. Because jobs in cities often pay more than farming, many people move from their villages to Phnom Penh or Siem Reap. They send part of their salary home to support their parents and younger brothers and sisters.", "words": [["farmer", "កសិករ"], ["factory", "រោងចក្រ"], ["industry", "ឧស្សាហកម្ម"], ["business", "អាជីវកម្ម"], ["salary", "ប្រាក់ខែ"], ["support", "ទ្រទ្រង់"]], "q": ["Why do many people move from villages to cities?", "What do they do with part of their salary?"], "a": ["Because jobs in cities often pay more than farming.", "They send it home to support their family."]}, {"n": 15, "t": "Life in the Rainy Season", "lv": "B2", "sec": "B", "body": "When the rainy season arrives, daily life in Cambodia changes noticeably. Almost every afternoon, dark clouds gather, and heavy rain falls for an hour or two, which turns dusty streets into muddy rivers. Farmers are delighted, because their rice fields need plenty of water to grow. In the countryside, many houses stand on tall wooden poles so that floodwater can pass underneath without damaging the family's home. In some provinces, the road to the market disappears under water, and people travel by small boat instead. Although the season brings difficulties, it also turns the landscape a brilliant green and fills the lakes and rivers that provide fish for the whole year.", "words": [["noticeably", "យ៉ាងច្បាស់"], ["muddy", "ភក់"], ["delighted", "សប្បាយចិត្ត"], ["poles", "បង្គោល"], ["floodwater", "ទឹកជំនន់"], ["landscape", "ទេសភាព"], ["brilliant", "ភ្លឺចែងចាំង"]], "q": ["Why are farmers happy in the rainy season?", "Why do some houses stand on tall poles?"], "a": ["Their rice fields need plenty of water to grow.", "So floodwater can pass underneath without damaging the home."]}, {"n": 16, "t": "Smartphones and Daily Life", "lv": "B2", "sec": "B", "body": "Over the last decade, the smartphone has become part of almost every Cambodian's daily routine. People use it to call their relatives, watch videos, listen to music and follow the news on social media. In many shops, customers pay by scanning a QR code with a banking app, which means they do not need to carry much cash. Students use phones to attend online classes and learn languages, while small business owners advertise their products and reply to customers directly. However, experts warn that spending too many hours on a screen can affect sleep, concentration and face-to-face relationships, so it is wise to use technology with balance.", "words": [["routine", "ទម្លាប់ប្រចាំថ្ងៃ"], ["scan", "ស្កែន"], ["cash", "ប្រាក់សុទ្ធ"], ["advertise", "ផ្សព្វផ្សាយ"], ["concentration", "ការផ្តោតអារម្មណ៍ "], ["balance", "តុល្យភាព"]], "q": ["How do many customers pay in shops?", "What problems can too much screen time cause?"], "a": ["By scanning a QR code with a banking app.", "It can affect sleep, concentration and face-to-face relationships."]}, {"n": 17, "t": "Our Planet, Earth", "lv": "A2", "sec": "C", "body": "Earth is the third planet from the Sun. It is the only planet we know where there is life. About seventy per cent of Earth is covered with water, so from space it looks blue. Earth also has an atmosphere, a layer of air that we need to breathe and that protects us from the strong light of the Sun. The planet turns once every twenty-four hours, which gives us day and night, and it goes around the Sun once every year, in about 365 days. Earth is about four and a half billion years old.", "words": [["planet", "ភព"], ["covered with", "គ្របដណ្តប់ដោយ"], ["atmosphere", "បរិយាកាស"], ["layer", "ស្រទាប់"], ["breathe", "ដកដង្ហើម"], ["billion", "ពាន់លាន"]], "q": ["How much of Earth is covered with water?", "How long does Earth take to go around the Sun?"], "a": ["About seventy per cent.", "About 365 days (one year)."]}, {"n": 18, "t": "The Sun", "lv": "A2", "sec": "C", "body": "The Sun is a star, and it is the center of our solar system. It is a giant ball of very hot gas. Without the Sun, there would be no light, no warmth and no life on Earth. Plants use sunlight to make food, and animals and people eat the plants or eat animals that eat plants. The Sun is about 150 million kilometres from Earth, but its light travels very fast and takes only about eight minutes to reach us. You should never look straight at the Sun, because it can hurt your eyes.", "words": [["star", "ផ្កាយ"], ["gas", "ឧស្ម័ន"], ["warmth", "ភាពកក់ក្តៅ "], ["reach", "ទៅដល់"], ["hurt", "ធ្វើឲ្យឈឺ"], ["centre", "ចំណុចកណ្តាល"]], "q": ["How long does sunlight take to reach Earth?", "Why should you not look straight at the Sun?"], "a": ["About eight minutes.", "It can hurt your eyes."]}, {"n": 19, "t": "The Moon", "lv": "B1", "sec": "C", "body": "The Moon is Earth's only natural satellite, and it is about 384,000 kilometres away. It does not make its own light; instead, we see it because it reflects the light of the Sun. As the Moon goes around Earth, which takes about a month, the part we can see changes, and this gives us the phases from a thin crescent to a full moon. Because the same side always faces us, no one saw the far side until a spacecraft took photos in 1959. In 1969, Neil Armstrong became the first person to walk on the Moon. The Moon's gravity also pulls on the oceans, which causes the tides.", "words": [["satellite", "ផ្កាយរណប"], ["reflect", "ឆ្លុះបញ្ចាំង"], ["crescent", "ព្រះចន្ទកន្លះ"], ["spacecraft", "យានអវកាស"], ["gravity", "ទំនាញ"], ["tide", "ជំនោរទឹក"]], "q": ["Why can we see the Moon?", "What causes the tides?"], "a": ["Because it reflects the light of the Sun.", "The Moon's gravity pulls on the oceans."]}, {"n": 20, "t": "The Solar System", "lv": "B1", "sec": "C", "body": "Our solar system consists of the Sun and everything that moves around it. There are eight planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune. The four planets closest to the Sun are small and rocky, while the four planets further away are much larger and are made mainly of gas or ice. Jupiter is the biggest planet, so large that all the other planets could fit inside it. Venus is the hottest planet because its thick atmosphere traps heat, and Saturn is famous for its beautiful rings. Scientists are still exploring the system with robots and telescopes.", "words": [["consist of", "មានផ្សំឡើងពី"], ["rocky", "ដុំថ្ម"], ["trap", "ចាប់ឃុំ"], ["ring", "ចិញ្ចៀន"], ["telescope", "តេឡេស្កុប"], ["explore", "ស្វែងរក"]], "q": ["How many planets are there in the solar system?", "Why is Venus the hottest planet?"], "a": ["Eight.", "Its thick atmosphere traps heat."]}, {"n": 21, "t": "Stars and Galaxies", "lv": "B1", "sec": "C", "body": "When you look at the sky on a clear night, you can see thousands of stars. Each one is a giant, glowing ball of gas, like our Sun, but most of them are so far away that they look like tiny dots. Astronomers measure these huge distances in light-years. One light-year is the distance light travels in a year, which is nearly ten trillion kilometres. The nearest star after the Sun, Proxima Centauri, is about four light-years away. Our galaxy, the Milky Way, contains at least a hundred billion stars, and it is only one of billions of galaxies in the universe.", "words": [["glowing", "ភ្លឺ"], ["astronomer", "តារាវិទូ"], ["distance", "គម្លាត"], ["light-year", "ឆ្នាំពន្លឺ"], ["galaxy", "កាឡាក់ស៊ី"], ["contain", "មាន"]], "q": ["What is a light-year?", "What is the name of our galaxy?"], "a": ["The distance that light travels in a year.", "The Milky Way."]}, {"n": 22, "t": "How Old Is the Universe?", "lv": "B2", "sec": "C", "body": "Most scientists believe that the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago in an event called the Big Bang. At that moment, everything we can observe today was packed into an extremely hot, tiny space, which then began to expand. Evidence for this theory comes from observations that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and from faint radiation left over from the early universe. Surprisingly, the matter that makes up stars, planets and people accounts for only about five per cent of the universe. The rest is made of dark matter and dark energy, which scientists cannot yet see directly and are still trying to understand.", "words": [["observe", "សង្កេត"], ["expand", "ពង្រីក"], ["evidence", "ភស្តុតាង"], ["faint", "ស្រអាប់"], ["radiation", "វិទ្យុសកម្ម"], ["account for", "មានចំនួន"], ["matter", "សារធាតុ"]], "q": ["How old is the universe, according to most scientists?", "How much of the universe is ordinary matter?"], "a": ["About 13.8 billion years old.", "Only about five per cent."]}, {"n": 23, "t": "Black Holes", "lv": "B2", "sec": "C", "body": "A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape from it. Most black holes form when a very massive star runs out of fuel and collapses under its own weight. The boundary around a black hole, beyond which there is no return, is called the event horizon. Because black holes do not give off light, scientists cannot photograph them directly; instead, they study how gas and stars behave nearby. In 2019, however, an international team of astronomers released the first image of a black hole, showing a glowing ring of gas around a dark centre, and a second image followed in 2022.", "words": [["escape", "គេចចេញ"], ["massive", "ធំសម្បើម"], ["collapse", "រលំ"], ["boundary", "ព្រំដែន"], ["release", "ចេញផ្សាយ"], ["fuel", "ឥន្ធនៈ"]], "q": ["Why can't light escape from a black hole?", "When was the first image of a black hole released?"], "a": ["Because gravity is so strong.", "In 2019."]}, {"n": 24, "t": "Our Changing Climate", "lv": "B2", "sec": "C", "body": "The world's average temperature has risen steadily over the past century, and scientists agree that human activity is the main cause. When we burn coal, oil and gas for electricity, transport and factories, we release gases that trap heat in the atmosphere. As a result, glaciers are melting, sea levels are rising, and extreme weather such as floods and droughts is becoming more common. Countries like Cambodia, where millions of people depend on rain for farming, are especially vulnerable. Fortunately, there are solutions: using solar and wind power, protecting forests, saving energy and choosing public transport. Individual actions may seem small, but together they can make a real difference.", "words": [["steadily", "ជាបន្តបន្ទាប់"], ["glacier", "ទឹកកកគ្របលើភ្នំ"], ["extreme", "ខ្លាំងបំផុត"], ["drought", "គ្រាំងស្ងួត"], ["vulnerable", "ងាយរងគ្រោះ"], ["renewable", "កកើតឡើងវិញ"]], "q": ["What is the main cause of the rising temperature?", "Name two solutions mentioned in the text."], "a": ["Human activity, especially burning coal, oil and gas.", "For example: solar and wind power, protecting forests, saving energy, public transport."]}, {"n": 25, "t": "Cambodia's Economy", "lv": "C1", "sec": "D", "body": "Over the past two decades, Cambodia has been one of the fastest-growing economies in Southeast Asia, driven mainly by garments, tourism and agriculture. Garment and footwear factories employ hundreds of thousands of workers, many of them young women from rural provinces, and their products are exported to markets in Europe and North America. Tourism, centred on Angkor, brought in billions of dollars before 2020. However, this rapid growth rests on a narrow base. When the pandemic closed borders and weakened consumer demand abroad, both tourism and garment orders suffered sharply, exposing how dependent the country was on a few industries and a handful of foreign markets. Economists therefore urge policymakers to diversify, by investing in skills, improving infrastructure and encouraging new sectors such as agribusiness and digital services. Whether Cambodia can make this transition will largely determine whether its recent prosperity proves durable.", "words": [["diversify", "ធ្វើឲ្យមានភាពចម្រុះ"], ["infrastructure", "ហេដ្ឋារចនាសម្ព័ន្ធ"], ["exposed", "ត្រូវបានបង្ហាញ"], ["durable", "ស្ថិតស្ថេរ"], ["policymaker", "អ្នកបង្កើតគោលនយោបាយ"]], "q": ["What weakness did the pandemic reveal?", "What does \"rests on a narrow base\" suggest?"], "a": ["The economy depended heavily on a few industries and foreign markets.", "That growth relies on only a few sources, so it is fragile."]}, {"n": 26, "t": "Angkor and the Mastery of Water", "lv": "C1", "sec": "D", "body": "At its height, Angkor was among the largest pre-industrial cities in the world, and its prosperity depended on mastering water. Engineers built vast reservoirs, known as barays, together with canals and embankments that stored the monsoon rains and distributed them to rice fields during the dry months. In recent years, laser mapping, known as lidar, has revealed that the urban network stretched much further beneath the forest than archaeologists had suspected. Yet the same sophisticated system may have been vulnerable. Evidence from tree rings points to severe droughts followed by intense monsoons in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, which could have damaged canals and reduced harvests. Scholars caution that climate alone cannot explain the decline; shifting trade routes, political conflict and changing religious practices probably played a part. Angkor's story is therefore a reminder that even advanced societies can be undermined when several pressures converge.", "words": [["reservoir", "អាងស្តុកទឹក"], ["embankment", "ទំនប់"], ["sophisticated", "ស្មុគស្មាញនិងទំនើប"], ["converge", "កើតមានព្រមគ្នា "], ["undermine", "ធ្វើឲ្យចុះខ្សោយ"]], "q": ["Why do scholars avoid blaming climate alone for Angkor's decline?", "What lesson does the author draw?"], "a": ["Other factors, such as trade, conflict and religious change, probably contributed.", "Even advanced societies can collapse when several pressures occur together."]}, {"n": 27, "t": "A City in a Hurry", "lv": "C1", "sec": "D", "body": "Phnom Penh has changed dramatically in little more than a generation. Glass towers, shopping malls and gated housing developments have appeared on land that was once rice fields, while lakes and wetlands on the city's edge have been filled with sand for construction. Supporters of this boom point to jobs, investment and a modern skyline, whereas critics warn that the city is losing green space, increasing its flood risk and displacing poorer residents, as the controversy over the filling of Boeung Kak lake illustrated. Meanwhile, buildings from the colonial era and the 1960s New Khmer Architecture movement are being demolished faster than they can be documented. Urban planners argue that development and heritage need not be enemies, provided that decisions are made transparently and with the participation of those they affect.", "words": [["wetland", "ដីសើម"], ["displace", "ធ្វើឲ្យបាត់ទីលំនៅ"], ["controversy", "ជម្លោះ"], ["demolish", "រុះរើ"], ["heritage", "បេតិកភណ្ឌ "], ["transparently", "ដោយតម្លាភាព"]], "q": ["What two views of the city's boom does the author present?", "What condition do planners give for combining development and heritage?"], "a": ["Supporters see jobs, investment and a modern skyline; critics see lost green space, flood risk and displaced residents.", "Decisions must be transparent and involve the people affected."]}, {"n": 28, "t": "Attention in the Smartphone Age", "lv": "C1", "sec": "D", "body": "Many people report that they find it harder to concentrate than they used to, and they often blame their phones. Researchers are cautious about blaming technology outright, but there is evidence that constant notifications fragment our attention: every interruption, however brief, forces the brain to switch tasks, and switching has a cost in time and accuracy. Social media platforms are designed to hold our attention for as long as possible, using endless scrolling and unpredictable rewards, which makes putting the phone down a matter of willpower rather than simple choice. Small habits can help, such as turning off non-essential alerts, keeping the phone out of the bedroom and setting aside blocks of time for focused work. The aim is not to reject technology but to use it deliberately instead of reflexively.", "words": [["fragment", "បំបែកជាកំណាត់"], ["outright", "ទាំងស្រុង"], ["notification", "ការជូនដំណឹង"], ["willpower", "កម្លាំងចិត្ត"], ["deliberately", "ដោយចេតនា"], ["reflexively", "ដោយឯកឯង"]], "q": ["Why is switching tasks costly?", "What does the author recommend?"], "a": ["It takes time and reduces accuracy each time the brain switches.", "Using technology deliberately: turning off alerts, keeping phones out of the bedroom, setting aside time for focused work."]}, {"n": 29, "t": "Worlds Beyond Our Own", "lv": "C1", "sec": "D", "body": "Until the 1990s, astronomers could only assume that planets existed around other stars. Today more than five thousand such worlds, called exoplanets, have been confirmed, and the number keeps growing. Many were found using the transit method: when a planet passes in front of its star, it blocks a tiny fraction of the starlight, and repeated dips in brightness reveal its presence. Some planets orbit in the so-called habitable zone, the region where temperatures might allow liquid water, although that alone does not guarantee life. The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, can analyse starlight that has filtered through a planet's atmosphere, searching for chemical clues such as water vapour or carbon dioxide. No one has yet found convincing evidence of life elsewhere, but for the first time the question can be examined with data rather than speculation.", "words": [["exoplanet", "ភពក្រៅប្រព័ន្ធព្រះអាទិត្យ"], ["transit", "ការឆ្លងកាត់"], ["habitable", "អាចរស់នៅបាន"], ["filter", "ត្រង"], ["vapour", "ចំហាយ"], ["speculation", "ការប៉ាន់ស្មាន"]], "q": ["How does the transit method work?", "Why does the author say the question of life can now be examined \"with data rather than speculation\"?"], "a": ["A planet passing in front of its star causes small, repeated dips in brightness.", "Because telescopes can now analyse the atmospheres of other planets for chemical clues."]}, {"n": 30, "t": "Looking into the Past", "lv": "C2", "sec": "D", "body": "When we gaze at a distant galaxy, we are not seeing it as it is but as it was. Light travels at roughly 300,000 kilometres per second, which sounds fast until one realises how vast the universe is: the journey can last billions of years. The Andromeda galaxy, for instance, appears to us as it looked about two and a half million years ago. The James Webb Space Telescope has detected galaxies whose light left them only a few hundred million years after the Big Bang, offering a glimpse of the universe in its infancy. In this sense, astronomers are historians as much as scientists: the farther they look, the further back in time they travel. The paradox is that no telescope can show us the present state of any distant object; we can only infer it from the evidence of its past, and the present, in the strict sense, remains forever out of reach.", "words": [["gaze", "សម្លឹង"], ["vast", "ធំធេង"], ["glimpse", "ការមើលឃើញមួយភ្លែត"], ["infancy", "វ័យតូច/ដំណាក់កាលដំបូង"], ["infer", "សន្និដ្ឋាន"], ["out of reach", "លើសពីដៃចាប់បាន"]], "q": ["In what sense are astronomers \"historians\"?", "What is the paradox mentioned?"], "a": ["Because by looking farther they are seeing earlier moments in time.", "We can never observe the current state of distant objects, only their past."]}, {"n": 31, "t": "Between Tradition and Change", "lv": "C2", "sec": "D", "body": "In Cambodia, as in many fast-changing societies, young people often find themselves between two worlds. At home, they are expected to honour their elders, observe religious customs and put the family's needs before their own; outside, they are exposed to global music, online communities and career paths that their grandparents could scarcely have imagined. It would be simplistic to portray this as a straightforward conflict between old and new. Many young Cambodians reinterpret tradition rather than abandon it: they livestream temple ceremonies for relatives abroad, mix classical melodies with contemporary beats, and open small businesses that sell traditional crafts through social media. Culture, after all, is not a museum piece to be preserved unchanged, but a living practice that survives precisely because each generation reshapes it. The real question is not whether tradition will change, but who will be allowed to decide how.", "words": [["scarcely", "ស្ទើរតែ"], ["simplistic", "សាមញ្ញពេក"], ["portray", "ពណ៌នា"], ["reinterpret", "បកស្រាយឡើងវិញ"], ["contemporary", "សម័យថ្មី"], ["precisely", "យ៉ាងពិតប្រាកដ"]], "q": ["Why does the author call a simple \"old versus new\" view simplistic?", "What does the final sentence imply?"], "a": ["Because many young people adapt tradition instead of rejecting it.", "The important issue is who has the authority to shape cultural change."]}, {"n": 32, "t": "The Burden of Being Beautiful", "lv": "C2", "sec": "D", "body": "Every year, millions of travellers are drawn to Angkor, and their spending sustains hotels, guides, drivers, restaurants and craftspeople in the nearby city of Siem Reap. Yet the very appeal of the site threatens it. Countless footsteps wear down ancient stone steps, hands darken carvings, and the growing demand for water has raised concerns about the stability of the sandy ground beneath the temples. Managers face a genuine dilemma: restricting visitor numbers would protect the monuments but would harm the livelihoods of those who depend on tourism. Responses so far have included limiting access to the most fragile areas, staggering crowds across a wider range of temples and encouraging travellers to stay longer and spend more thoughtfully. None of these measures is perfect, but they share an insight worth remembering: heritage that is loved to death helps no one, and its protection is, ultimately, a collective responsibility.", "words": [["sustain", "ទ្រទ្រង់"], ["carving", "ចម្លាក់"], ["dilemma", "ជម្រើសពិបាក"], ["livelihood", "មុខរបរចិញ្ចឹមជីវិត"], ["stagger", "ចែកចាយឲ្យមិនកកកុញ"], ["ultimately", "នៅទីបំផុត"]], "q": ["What dilemma do the managers face?", "What does \"loved to death\" mean here?"], "a": ["Limiting visitors protects the monuments but harms people who depend on tourism.", "Excessive visits can damage a heritage site until it is destroyed."]}, {"n": 33, "t": "The Language We Think In", "lv": "C2", "sec": "D", "body": "Does the language we speak shape the way we think? The idea, often called linguistic relativity, has divided scholars for decades. In its strong form, which claims that language determines thought, it has largely been rejected; in a weaker form, however, it enjoys considerable support. Languages direct attention to different details: Khmer verbs, for example, do not change their form to show tense, so speakers rely on time words and context, whereas English speakers must mark every verb with a time frame. Learners often notice this when they forget an \"-ed\" or an \"-s\", not because they cannot think about the past but because their first language never demanded it. Bilingual people frequently report that they feel like slightly different persons in each language, more formal in one, more playful in another. Such anecdotes prove nothing on their own, but they remind us that a language is not merely a tool for labelling reality; it is a lens through which we habitually look.", "words": [["linguistic", "ភាសាវិទ្យា"], ["considerable", "ច្រើនគួរសម"], ["tense", "កាល"], ["anecdote", "រឿងប្រាប់ពីបទពិសោធន៍"], ["merely", "តែប៉ុណ្ណោះ"], ["habitually", "ជាទម្លាប់"]], "q": ["How does the author explain why learners forget \"-ed\" or \"-s\"?", "Why does the author say anecdotes \"prove nothing on their own\"?"], "a": ["Their first language, Khmer, does not require verbs to show tense, so it is not a habit.", "Personal stories are not scientific evidence, although they suggest ideas worth investigating."]}, {"n": 34, "t": "Are We Alone?", "lv": "C2", "sec": "D", "body": "The Milky Way contains hundreds of billions of stars, and the observable universe holds billions of galaxies. Given those numbers, many scientists find it hard to believe that Earth is the only place where life has appeared. And yet our telescopes have detected no unmistakable signal, no message and no visitor. This tension is known as the Fermi paradox, after the physicist Enrico Fermi, who is said to have asked the simple question, \"Where is everybody?\" Explanations vary widely. Perhaps life is common but intelligent life is extraordinarily rare; perhaps civilisations tend to destroy themselves before they can travel between stars; perhaps the distances are so great that contact is practically impossible; or perhaps we have simply not searched for long enough. The silence is open to many readings, and each reveals as much about our own hopes and fears as about the cosmos itself.", "words": [["observable", "អាចសង្កេតបាន"], ["unmistakable", "មិនអាចច្រឡំបាន"], ["tension", "ភាពផ្ទុយគ្នា "], ["civilisation", "អារ្យធម៌"], ["extraordinarily", "ខុសពីធម្មតា"], ["cosmos", "សកលលោក"]], "q": ["What contradiction does the Fermi paradox describe?", "What does the final sentence suggest about the explanations?"], "a": ["Statistically life seems likely, but we have found no evidence of it.", "They reflect human attitudes, hopes and fears as much as scientific facts."]}], "paras": [{"id": "E1", "t": "Kampot", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Kampot is a quiet town on a river in the south of Cambodia. The streets are calm, and many old buildings were built during the French period. Around the town, farmers grow pepper and durian, and workers collect salt from large fields near the sea. In the evening, visitors sit by the river and watch the sun go down behind the mountains. Many people come here to relax for a few days.", "words": [["pepper", "ម្ទេស"], ["salt", "អំបិល"], ["relax", "សម្រាក"]]}, {"id": "E2", "t": "Kep", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Kep is a small seaside town known for its crab market. Fishermen catch crabs in the morning, and cooks prepare them fresh for lunch. A popular dish is crab fried with green Kampot pepper. After lunch, people take a short boat ride to Rabbit Island, where the water is clear and the beach is quiet. Kep is a good place for families who like seafood and the sea.", "words": [["crab", "ក្តាម"], ["seafood", "អាហារសមុទ្រ"], ["fisherman", "អ្នកនេសាទ"]]}, {"id": "E3", "t": "Battambang", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Battambang is one of the biggest cities in Cambodia, and it is surrounded by rice fields. It is known for its old colonial buildings, its relaxed pace and its young artists. At a local arts school, children learn painting, music and circus skills, and the performers now travel abroad to show their talent. A visit to Battambang shows another side of Cambodia: quieter than the capital, but full of creative energy.", "words": [["surrounded", "ព័ទ្ធជុំវិញ"], ["circus", "សៀក"], ["creative", "មានភាពច្នៃប្រឌិត"]]}, {"id": "E4", "t": "River Dolphins at Kratie", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "In the Mekong River near the town of Kratie, visitors can see rare Irrawaddy dolphins. These grey animals have round heads and appear briefly at the surface to breathe. There are now fewer than one hundred of them in this part of the river, mainly because of fishing nets, pollution and changes to the river. Local communities and conservation groups are working together to protect them, and tourism provides some of the money for this work.", "words": [["dolphin", "ផ្សោត"], ["pollution", "ការបំពុល"], ["conservation", "ការអភិរក្ស"]]}, {"id": "E5", "t": "Ratanakiri", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Ratanakiri, in the north-east of Cambodia, is a province of red soil, forests and waterfalls. Many different ethnic groups live here, each with its own language and traditions, and they have lived in the highlands for centuries. One of the most popular places to visit is Yeak Laom, a perfectly round lake in an old volcanic crater, where people swim in clear, cool water. The province is a good example of how rich and varied the country's culture is.", "words": [["waterfall", "ទឹកធ្លាក់"], ["ethnic", "ជនជាតិ"], ["crater", "មាត់ភ្នំភ្លើង"]]}, {"id": "E6", "t": "The Cardamom Mountains", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "In the south-west of Cambodia, the Cardamom Mountains hold one of the largest areas of rainforest in Southeast Asia. The forest is home to elephants, sun bears and hundreds of species of birds, many of which exist nowhere else. Because it also absorbs carbon dioxide and regulates rainfall, the forest benefits people who live far from it. Rangers and local villagers patrol the area to prevent illegal logging, and small ecotourism projects offer an income that does not depend on cutting trees.", "words": [["rainforest", "ព្រៃត្រូពិច"], ["species", "ប្រភេទសត្វ"], ["logging", "ការកាប់បំផ្លាញព្រៃ"]]}, {"id": "E7", "t": "Cambodian Rice", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Rice is more than food in Cambodia; it is part of daily life. Farmers plant it when the rains begin, and they cut it a few months later, usually working together as a family. Cambodia grows many kinds of rice, and one fragrant type has won international prizes. People eat rice at almost every meal, and in Khmer the word for \"to eat\" is often used to mean \"to eat rice\". A good harvest makes a whole village happy.", "words": [["fragrant", "ក្រអូប"], ["prize", "រង្វាន់"], ["grow", "ដាំដុះ"]]}, {"id": "E8", "t": "The Krama", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "The krama is a checked cotton scarf that almost every Cambodian owns. It is light and cheap, and it has many uses. Farmers wear it on their heads to stay cool, mothers tie a baby to their back with it, and children use it as a towel after swimming. It can also be a belt, a bag or a simple hat. For many people, the krama is a symbol of Khmer identity.", "words": [["scarf", "កន្សែង"], ["checked", "ក្រឡា"], ["identity", "អត្តសញ្ញាណ"]]}, {"id": "E9", "t": "Silk Weaving", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "In villages near Takeo and Siem Reap, women weave silk on wooden looms, a skill that has been passed from mother to daughter for generations. First, they feed silkworms with mulberry leaves; then they collect the thread from the cocoons and colour it with natural dyes. Making a single piece of cloth can take weeks, which explains why handmade silk is expensive. Buying from these villages helps keep an old craft alive and supports local families.", "words": [["weave", "ត្បាញ"], ["loom", "ទ្រនាប់ត្បាញ"], ["thread", "អំបោះ"]]}, {"id": "E10", "t": "Pchum Ben", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Pchum Ben is a religious festival in September or October, when Cambodians remember family members who have died. For fifteen days, people visit pagodas early in the morning and offer rice and food to monks, believing that the food also reaches their ancestors. Many families travel to their home villages so they can pray together. Although it is a serious time, it is also a moment for relatives to meet and strengthen their bonds.", "words": [["ancestor", "បុព្វបុរស"], ["pray", "បួងសួង"], ["bond", "ចំណងសាមគ្គី"]]}, {"id": "E11", "t": "A Cambodian Wedding", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "A traditional Cambodian wedding is colourful and full of ceremony. The bride and groom change clothes several times during the day, wearing bright silk costumes. Monks give blessings, and relatives tie red threads around the couple's wrists to wish them good luck. In the evening, guests enjoy a big meal and give small envelopes of money to help the new family. Today, many weddings are shorter than before, but the main customs remain.", "words": [["bride", "កូនក្រមុំ"], ["groom", "កូនកំលោះ"], ["envelope", "ស្រោមសំបុត្រ"]]}, {"id": "E12", "t": "The Khmer Language", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Khmer is the official language of Cambodia, and about sixteen million people speak it. Its alphabet, which came originally from India, is one of the longest in the world, with many consonants and vowels. Unlike English, Khmer does not change the verb for past, present or future; speakers add time words instead. Learning to read Khmer takes patience, but many foreign students enjoy discovering its beautiful, curved letters.", "words": [["official", "ជាផ្លូវការ"], ["consonant", "ព្យញ្ជនៈ"], ["vowel", "ស្រៈ"]]}, {"id": "E13", "t": "The Islands of the South", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Off the coast near Sihanoukville, there are islands with white sand and clear blue water, such as Koh Rong and Koh Rong Sanloem. Visitors travel there by boat and stay in small wooden huts near the beach. During the day, they swim, snorkel and walk through the forest; at night, they watch the stars, because there are few lights. For many travellers, the islands are the perfect place to rest after a long trip.", "words": [["island", "កោះ"], ["snorkel", "ហែលទឹកមើលត្រី"], ["hut", "រោង"]]}, {"id": "E14", "t": "Cambodian Music", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Traditional Cambodian music uses instruments such as the roneat, a wooden xylophone, and the tro, a two-stringed fiddle. These sounds are often heard at ceremonies, weddings and in temples. In the 1960s, singers such as Sinn Sisamouth made modern Khmer songs that mixed local melodies with rock and pop from abroad, and many people still love those songs today. Now, young artists continue the story by combining old rhythms with new styles.", "words": [["instrument", "ឧបករណ៍ភ្លេង"], ["melody", "បទភ្លេង"], ["rhythm", "ចង្វាក់"]]}, {"id": "E15", "t": "Wildlife of Cambodia", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Cambodia", "body": "Cambodia has many kinds of animals and birds. The giant ibis, a tall bird with a long curved beak, is the national bird, and it lives in the northern forests. In the rivers, people can find fish, turtles and dolphins; in the mountains, there are elephants, monkeys and snakes. Because forests are shrinking, some animals are now rare, so it is important to protect their homes.", "words": [["beak", "ចំពុះ"], ["turtle", "អណ្តើក"], ["shrink", "រួមតូច"]]}, {"id": "F1", "t": "Waking Up Early", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "In many Cambodian homes, the day starts early. At five thirty, a rooster sings outside, and the first motorbikes start in the street. Parents get up to cook rice, and children wash their faces and put on their uniforms. Some people go to the pagoda to give food to the monks. By six thirty, the street is already full of sound and movement. Even though it is early, the air is fresh, and most people start the day with a smile.", "words": [["rooster", "មាន់ញី/មាន់ឈ្មោល"], ["pagoda", "វត្ត"], ["movement", "ចលនា"]]}, {"id": "F2", "t": "Cooking Rice at Home", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Rice is on the table at almost every meal, so many families have a rice cooker at home. First, you wash the rice two or three times in cold water. Then you add water, close the lid and press the button. After twenty minutes, the rice is soft and ready to eat. In the past, people cooked rice in a pot on a wood fire, but today most people prefer the easy way.", "words": [["lid", "គម្រប"], ["soft", "ទន់"], ["prefer", "ចូលចិត្តជាង"]]}, {"id": "F3", "t": "Street Food in the Evening", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "When the sun goes down, small food stalls open along the street. A woman sells grilled meat on sticks, a man cooks noodles in a big pan, and another seller has sweet drinks with ice. People sit on low plastic chairs and eat while they talk. The food is cheap, fresh and tasty, and the smell makes everybody hungry. For many workers, this is the best part of the day.", "words": [["stall", "តូប"], ["stick", "ដំបង"], ["tasty", "ឆ្ងាញ់"]]}, {"id": "F4", "t": "A Day at the Office", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Sokunthea works in a small company in Phnom Penh. She arrives at eight, checks her email and writes a list of things to do. In the morning, she has a meeting with her team to discuss a new project. At noon, she eats lunch with her colleagues at a nearby restaurant. In the afternoon, she answers customers' questions and prepares a report. When she gets home, she is tired but satisfied, because she feels she has learned something new.", "words": [["colleague", "មិត្តរួមការងារ"], ["project", "គម្រោង"], ["satisfied", "ពេញចិត្ត"]]}, {"id": "F5", "t": "Housework", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "On Saturday morning, the whole family cleans the house. Father washes the motorbike, mother does the laundry, and the children sweep the floor and wash the dishes. They open all the windows to let in fresh air. The clothes dry quickly in the sun, and the house smells clean. After the work, everybody feels proud, and mother prepares a special lunch to thank them.", "words": [["laundry", "ការបោកគក់"], ["sweep", "បោស"], ["dishes", "ចាន"]]}, {"id": "F6", "t": "A Visit to the Pagoda", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "On the holy days of the month, many Cambodians visit their local pagoda. They bring flowers, incense and food for the monks, and they kneel in front of the Buddha statue to pray. Older people often stay for the whole day, listening to the monks' chanting and sharing a meal with friends. For many, the pagoda is not only a place of worship, but also a school, a community centre and a place of peace.", "words": [["incense", "ធូប"], ["kneel", "លុតជង្គង់"], ["worship", "ការគោរពបូជា"]]}, {"id": "F7", "t": "Weekend with Friends", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "On Sunday, I meet my friends at a small café near the river. We order iced coffee, share a plate of fried banana and talk for hours. Sometimes we play cards, and sometimes we take photos of the sunset. In the evening, we walk to the night market to look for cheap clothes and snacks. By nine o'clock, we are tired but happy, and I go home by tuk-tuk.", "words": [["share", "ចែករំលែក"], ["sunset", "ថ្ងៃលិច"], ["snack", "នំចំណី "]]}, {"id": "F8", "t": "Going to the Doctor", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Last week, I had a high fever and a bad cough, so I went to a clinic. The nurse measured my temperature and asked me to wait. Then the doctor listened to my chest, looked at my throat and asked several questions about my symptoms. She said I had an infection and gave me a prescription for some medicine. She also told me to drink a lot of water and rest for three days. Two days later, I felt much better.", "words": [["fever", "គ្រុនក្តៅ "], ["symptom", "រោគសញ្ញា "], ["prescription", "វេជ្ជបញ្ជា "]]}, {"id": "F9", "t": "The Hot Season", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "In March and April, Cambodia is very hot. At noon, the sun is strong, and the streets are empty. People stay in the shade, drink cold water and eat fruit like watermelon and mango. Some shops close for two hours to let the workers rest. In the evening, when the air becomes cooler, everybody comes out again, and the city feels alive.", "words": [["shade", "ម្លប់"], ["watermelon", "ឪឡឹក"], ["alive", "មានជីវិតរស់រវើក"]]}, {"id": "F10", "t": "A Haircut", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Yesterday, my hair was too long, so I went to a hair salon near my house. The hairdresser washed my hair and asked, \"How would you like it?\" I showed her a photo on my phone. She cut it carefully and used a dryer to finish. The haircut cost three dollars. When I looked in the mirror, I smiled, because I looked fresh and new.", "words": [["hairdresser", "ជាងកាត់សក់"], ["mirror", "កញ្ចក់"], ["dryer", "ម៉ាស៊ីនសម្ងួតសក់"]]}, {"id": "F11", "t": "Learning English", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Learning English is not easy, but it is possible if you practise every day. A good student reads a little, listens to a little and speaks a little, even when he feels shy. Mistakes are normal; they show that you are trying. After a few months, you start to notice progress: you understand a song, you answer a question quickly, or you write a message without a dictionary. These small victories give you the energy to continue.", "words": [["shy", "អៀនខ្មាស"], ["progress", "ភាពរីកចម្រើន"], ["victory", "ជ័យជម្នះ"]]}, {"id": "F12", "t": "Saving Money", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Many young Cambodians want to save money for the future, but it can be difficult when prices keep rising. A simple method is to put aside a small amount at the beginning of each month, before spending on anything else. Some people also keep a notebook to record what they buy, which helps them notice unnecessary costs. Even a small habit, repeated for years, can pay for a course, a motorbike or the start of a business.", "words": [["save", "សន្សំ"], ["expense", "ការចំណាយ"], ["habit", "ទម្លាប់"]]}, {"id": "F13", "t": "A Power Cut", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "Last night, the electricity suddenly went out in the middle of dinner. At first, everyone laughed, then somebody lit a candle, and the room became warm and quiet. Without television or phones, we had to talk to each other, and my grandmother told stories from her childhood that I had never heard before. When the light returned two hours later, nobody moved to turn on the television. It was an unexpectedly pleasant evening.", "words": [["electricity", "អគ្គិសនី"], ["candle", "ទៀន"], ["unexpectedly", "ដោយមិននឹកស្មាន"]]}, {"id": "F14", "t": "Good Neighbours", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "In Cambodian neighbourhoods, people often know the names of everyone on their street. When someone is ill, the neighbours bring soup; when there is a wedding or a funeral, they help to cook and carry tables. Children move freely between houses, and no one is surprised to find an extra child at the dinner table. This sense of community can be hard to find in big cities, which is why many people say that good neighbours are worth more than expensive gifts.", "words": [["neighbourhood", "សង្កាត់/ភូមិ"], ["funeral", "បុណ្យសព"], ["community", "សហគមន៍"]]}, {"id": "F15", "t": "Evening Rain", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Daily Life", "body": "The first drops fall on the tin roof like small stones, and within seconds the sound becomes a steady roar. Children run inside, laughing, while the motorbikes stop under trees and shop awnings. The smell of wet earth rises from the street, and the heat of the day finally breaks. Inside the house, mother turns on the lights and begins to make tea, and the whole family gathers close to the open door to watch the rain.", "words": [["roar", "សំឡេងគ្រហឹម"], ["awning", "ដំបូលបាំងភ្លៀង"], ["gather", "ជួបជុំ"]]}, {"id": "G1", "t": "The Night Sky", "lv": "A2", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "On a clear night in the countryside, the sky is full of stars. Far from the city lights, you can see the Moon, bright planets and even a pale white band called the Milky Way. Children lie on a mat and try to count the stars, but there are too many. Farmers in the past used the stars to know when to plant rice. Looking at the night sky makes people feel small, but also calm.", "words": [["pale", "ស្លេក"], ["band", "ក្រុម/ឆ្នូត"], ["count", "រាប់"]]}, {"id": "G2", "t": "Day and Night", "lv": "A2", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Earth turns like a ball on its axis, and one full turn takes twenty-four hours. When your part of Earth faces the Sun, it is day; when it turns away, it is night. That is why, when it is morning in Cambodia, it is evening in the United States. Because of this, friends in different countries must check the time before they make a phone call.", "words": [["axis", "អ័ក្ស"], ["turn", "វិល"], ["face", "បែរមុខទៅរក"]]}, {"id": "G3", "t": "Why Do We Have Seasons?", "lv": "A2", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "In many countries, the weather changes through the year: spring, summer, autumn and winter. This happens because Earth is tilted as it goes around the Sun. When the northern half leans towards the Sun, it is summer there and winter in the south. Cambodia is close to the equator, so it does not have four seasons, but it has a rainy season and a dry season instead.", "words": [["tilted", "ទ្រេត"], ["equator", "អេក្វាទ័រ"], ["lean", "ផ្អៀង"]]}, {"id": "G4", "t": "Mars, the Red Planet", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Mars looks red because its soil contains a lot of iron, which rusts like old metal. The planet is smaller and colder than Earth, and its air is very thin, so people could not breathe there. However, it has the biggest volcano in the solar system and a huge canyon, and there is evidence that liquid water once flowed on its surface. Robotic vehicles, called rovers, are now exploring the ground and collecting samples, and many scientists hope that humans will visit one day.", "words": [["rust", "ច្រែះ"], ["canyon", "ជ្រោះ"], ["rover", "យានរុករក"]]}, {"id": "G5", "t": "Jupiter", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Jupiter is the largest planet in our solar system, and it is made mostly of hydrogen and helium rather than solid rock. Its surface is covered with colourful stripes of cloud and a giant storm called the Great Red Spot, which is larger than Earth and has lasted for hundreds of years. Jupiter also has dozens of moons. Because of its strong gravity, it attracts many comets and asteroids, which may protect smaller planets like ours.", "words": [["hydrogen", "អ៊ីដ្រូសែន"], ["stripe", "ឆ្នូត"], ["asteroid", "អាស្ទេរ៉ូអ៊ីត"]]}, {"id": "G6", "t": "The Rings of Saturn", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Saturn is famous for its rings, which can be seen even with a small telescope. They are not solid; they are made of billions of pieces of ice and rock, from tiny grains to blocks as big as houses, all moving around the planet. Although the rings stretch for thousands of kilometres, they are extremely thin, in some places only a few metres thick. Scientists are still debating how and when they formed.", "words": [["grain", "គ្រាប់"], ["stretch", "លាតសន្ធឹង"], ["thick", "ក្រាស់"]]}, {"id": "G7", "t": "Eclipses", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth and covers the Sun for a few minutes. For a short time, the day becomes dark, birds go quiet, and the temperature falls. A lunar eclipse is different: it happens when Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon, and the Moon turns a dark red colour. In the past, people were afraid of eclipses, but today astronomers can predict them years in advance.", "words": [["eclipse", "គ្រាស"], ["predict", "ទស្សន៍ទាយ/ព្យាករណ៍ "], ["in advance", "ជាមុន"]]}, {"id": "G8", "t": "Comets", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "A comet is a ball of ice, dust and rock that travels around the Sun in a long orbit. When it comes close to the Sun, the heat turns some of the ice into gas, and a bright tail appears that always points away from the Sun. The most famous comet, Halley's Comet, returns about every seventy-six years, and it will be visible again in 2061. For ancient people, comets were mysterious signs in the sky.", "words": [["orbit", "គន្លង"], ["tail", "កន្ទុយ"], ["mysterious", "អាថ៌កំបាំង"]]}, {"id": "G9", "t": "Shooting Stars", "lv": "A2", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "A shooting star is not really a star. It is a tiny piece of rock, usually smaller than a grain of rice, that enters Earth's air at very high speed. The rock becomes so hot that it burns and makes a short line of light in the sky, which lasts only a second. On some nights of the year, you can see dozens of them every hour. Many people make a wish when they see one.", "words": [["shooting star", "ផ្កាយរសាត់"], ["wish", "បំណងប្រាថ្នា "], ["speed", "ល្បឿន"]]}, {"id": "G10", "t": "Our Home Galaxy", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "The Milky Way is a spiral galaxy, which means that its stars are arranged in long, curved arms around a bright centre. Our Sun lies in one of these arms, about two-thirds of the way out from the middle. The Sun and the planets travel around the centre at a speed of hundreds of kilometres per second, yet one full orbit takes roughly 230 million years. When the dinosaurs lived, our solar system was on the opposite side of the galaxy.", "words": [["spiral", "រាងវង់"], ["arrange", "រៀបចំ"], ["opposite", "ផ្ទុយ"]]}, {"id": "G11", "t": "Gravity", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Gravity is the force that pulls objects towards each other. It keeps us on the ground, makes rain fall and holds the Moon in orbit around Earth. According to a famous story, Isaac Newton began to think about gravity when he saw an apple fall from a tree. Astronauts in the space station seem to float, not because there is no gravity, but because they are constantly falling around Earth together with the station.", "words": [["force", "កម្លាំង"], ["astronaut", "អវកាសយានិក"], ["float", "អណ្តែត"]]}, {"id": "G12", "t": "Exploring Space", "lv": "B2", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Humans entered the space age in 1957, when the Soviet Union launched the first artificial satellite, and four years later Yuri Gagarin became the first person to orbit Earth. In 1969, astronauts landed on the Moon, an achievement that still inspires people today. Since then, scientists from many nations have cooperated to build the International Space Station, and private companies have begun to launch rockets as well. Critics ask whether money spent on space would be better used on Earth, but supporters reply that the technology developed for space often improves daily life.", "words": [["artificial", "សិប្បនិម្មិត"], ["achievement", "សមិទ្ធផល"], ["cooperate", "សហការ"]]}, {"id": "G13", "t": "The Oceans", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Oceans cover more than seventy per cent of the planet, and they hold almost all of Earth's water. They also produce much of the oxygen we breathe and help control the climate by storing heat. The deepest place, the Mariana Trench, is nearly eleven kilometres below the surface, and humans have explored only a small part of the deep sea. Pollution, plastic and overfishing now threaten ocean life, so many countries are creating protected areas.", "words": [["oxygen", "អុកស៊ីសែន"], ["threaten", "គំរាមកំហែង"], ["protected", "ត្រូវបានការពារ"]]}, {"id": "G14", "t": "Rainforests", "lv": "B1", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Rainforests grow in warm, wet parts of the world, and they are home to an enormous variety of plants and animals. The tall trees store large amounts of carbon, produce moisture that returns as rain and shelter many species that exist nowhere else. Many medicines come from forest plants. Sadly, farmers and companies are cutting down forests at a fast rate, so protecting them is important for the climate as well as for wildlife.", "words": [["variety", "ភាពចម្រុះ"], ["moisture", "សំណើម"], ["wildlife", "សត្វព្រៃ"]]}, {"id": "G15", "t": "The Water Cycle", "lv": "A2", "grp": "The Universe and Nature", "body": "Water never disappears; it moves in a circle. The Sun heats the oceans, rivers and lakes, and water rises into the air as invisible vapour. High in the sky, the vapour cools and becomes small drops, which form clouds. When the drops become heavy, they fall as rain. The rain flows into rivers and the sea, and the story begins again. In Cambodia, this cycle brings the heavy rains of the monsoon.", "words": [["vapour", "ចំហាយ"], ["invisible", "មើលមិនឃើញ"], ["cycle", "វដ្ត"]]}, {"id": "H1", "t": "Healthy Habits", "lv": "A2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "A healthy life starts with simple habits. Drink enough water, eat fruit and vegetables every day, and try to go to bed before eleven. Walk or ride a bicycle instead of taking a motorbike for short trips. Wash your hands before meals. These actions are easy and cheap, but together they can make you stronger and give you more energy for study and work.", "words": [["habit", "ទម្លាប់"], ["energy", "ថាមពល"], ["stronger", "ខ្លាំងជាង"]]}, {"id": "H2", "t": "Why Sleep Matters", "lv": "B1", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep every night. During sleep, the body repairs itself, and the brain sorts the information it received during the day, which is why students who sleep well often remember more. People who sleep badly feel tired, become angry more easily and find it harder to concentrate. To improve sleep, try to go to bed at the same time each day and avoid screens an hour before bedtime.", "words": [["repair", "ជួសជុល"], ["concentrate", "ផ្តោតអារម្មណ៍ "], ["avoid", "ចៀសវាង"]]}, {"id": "H3", "t": "Exercise", "lv": "A2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Exercise does not have to be difficult. In the morning or evening, many Cambodians walk in the park, play badminton or take a group aerobics class by the river. After half an hour, you feel warm, happy and more awake. Exercise is good for your heart, your muscles and your mood. If you do not have much time, just walk quickly for twenty minutes a day.", "words": [["muscle", "សាច់ដុំ"], ["mood", "អារម្មណ៍ "], ["heart", "បេះដូង"]]}, {"id": "H4", "t": "Eating Well", "lv": "B1", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "A balanced diet includes different kinds of food: rice or bread for energy, fish, eggs or beans for protein, and plenty of fresh vegetables and fruit for vitamins. It is wise to eat less sugar and fried food, because too much of them can cause health problems. Cooking at home is usually healthier than eating outside, and it also lets you control how much oil and salt you use.", "words": [["balanced", "មានតុល្យភាព"], ["vitamin", "វីតាមីន"], ["sugar", "ស្ករ"]]}, {"id": "H5", "t": "Working from Home", "lv": "B1", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Since the pandemic, more people work from home for part of the week. The main advantage is that they save time and money on travelling, and they can organise their day more flexibly. However, there are difficulties as well: it is easy to feel lonely, and the line between work and rest becomes blurred. Experts suggest keeping a regular schedule, setting up a quiet corner for work and taking breaks to stretch and talk to friends.", "words": [["advantage", "អត្ថប្រយោជន៍"], ["flexibly", "ដោយបត់បែន"], ["lonely", "ឯកោ"]]}, {"id": "H6", "t": "A Job Interview", "lv": "B1", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Before a job interview, it is important to learn about the company, prepare answers to common questions and dress neatly. On the day, arrive ten minutes early and greet everyone politely. When the interviewer asks, \"Tell me about yourself,\" give a short, clear answer about your studies, skills and goals. Do not be afraid to say that you do not know something; honesty and a willingness to learn are valued by most employers.", "words": [["interview", "ការសម្ភាសន៍"], ["employer", "និយោជក"], ["willingness", "ឆន្ទៈ"]]}, {"id": "H7", "t": "Teamwork", "lv": "B2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Most modern jobs require people to work in teams, and a good team is more than a group of skilled individuals. Members need to listen to each other, share information honestly and accept criticism without taking it personally. When a conflict appears, the best response is to discuss the problem calmly and look for a solution that satisfies everyone. Studies suggest that teams with trust between members perform better, because people feel safe to suggest ideas and admit mistakes.", "words": [["criticism", "ការរិះគន់"], ["conflict", "ជម្លោះ"], ["trust", "ការជឿទុកចិត្ត"]]}, {"id": "H8", "t": "Flying for the First Time", "lv": "A2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "At the airport, you show your ticket and passport, leave your big bag at the desk and walk to the gate. The plane is large, and the seats are in long rows. When it takes off, you feel the engines and see the city become small. The flight attendants bring water and food, and you can watch films or look through the window. After landing, you collect your bag and walk out, a little tired but excited.", "words": [["passport", "លិខិតឆ្លងដែន"], ["gate", "ច្រកចូល"], ["take off", "ហោះឡើង"]]}, {"id": "H9", "t": "Living in a New Culture", "lv": "B2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Moving to another country is exciting, but it is also demanding. At first, everything seems fascinating: the food, the buildings and the customs. After a few weeks, however, many people feel confused or homesick, because small differences, such as how people greet each other or show respect, become tiring. This stage is normal, and it usually passes as you learn the language and make friends. Those who stay curious and humble tend to adapt best.", "words": [["demanding", "តម្រូវឲ្យមានការខំប្រឹង"], ["homesick", "នឹករលឹកផ្ទះ"], ["humble", "បន្ទាបខ្លួន"]]}, {"id": "H10", "t": "Plastic Waste", "lv": "B1", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Plastic is cheap and useful, but it creates a serious problem because it does not disappear. A plastic bottle can stay in the environment for hundreds of years, and much of it ends up in rivers and then in the sea. Animals sometimes mistake plastic for food, and tiny pieces enter the food chain. You can help by bringing your own bag to the market, refusing plastic straws and sorting your rubbish for recycling.", "words": [["waste", "កាកសំណល់"], ["recycle", "កែច្នៃឡើងវិញ"], ["straw", "បំពង់ជញ្ជក់"]]}, {"id": "H11", "t": "Clean Energy", "lv": "B2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "For more than a century, most electricity has come from burning coal, oil and gas, which release gases that warm the planet. Today, solar panels and wind turbines are becoming cheaper, and in many countries they produce power at a lower cost than fossil fuels. The challenge is that the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow, so engineers are developing better batteries to store energy. If this succeeds, cities of the future may run on clean power.", "words": [["turbine", "ទួរប៊ីន"], ["battery", "ថ្ម"], ["fossil fuel", "ឥន្ធនៈហ្វូស៊ីល"]]}, {"id": "H12", "t": "A True Friend", "lv": "A2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "A true friend listens when you are sad and laughs with you when you are happy. He tells you the truth, even when it is not easy to hear, and he helps you when you have a problem. You may not meet every day, but when you do, it feels like no time has passed. In life, you do not need many friends; a few good ones are enough.", "words": [["truth", "ការពិត"], ["sad", "កំសត់/ក្រៀមក្រំ"], ["enough", "គ្រប់គ្រាន់"]]}, {"id": "H13", "t": "Managing Your Time", "lv": "B2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "The feeling of having too little time is common, yet the problem is often not the quantity of time but the way we use it. A useful method is to list tomorrow's tasks every evening and mark the two or three that really matter. Do those first, when your energy is highest, and leave the less important ones for later. It also helps to say no to requests that do not fit your goals, because every \"yes\" to something is a \"no\" to something else.", "words": [["quantity", "បរិមាណ"], ["task", "កិច្ចការ"], ["request", "សំណើ "]]}, {"id": "H14", "t": "The Internet", "lv": "B1", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "The internet connects billions of people, and it has changed the way we learn, work and communicate. In a few seconds, you can search for information, send a message to a friend in another country or watch a lecture from a famous university. Yet not everything online is true, so it is important to check the source and compare several websites before you believe a surprising claim. Used carefully, the internet is one of the best tools for learning.", "words": [["connect", "តភ្ជាប់"], ["source", "ប្រភព"], ["claim", "ការអះអាង"]]}, {"id": "H15", "t": "Why Do We Dream?", "lv": "B2", "grp": "World, Health, Work and Travel", "body": "Everyone dreams, although many people forget their dreams as soon as they wake up. Most vivid dreams occur during a stage of sleep called REM, when the brain is very active, but the body is almost paralysed. Scientists have several theories: dreams may help us process emotions, strengthen memories or simply result from random brain activity. At present, no single explanation has been proved, and that is part of what makes dreams so fascinating.", "words": [["vivid", "ច្បាស់ក្លា "], ["paralysed", "ស្ពឹកស្លាប់"], ["theory", "ទ្រឹស្តី"]]}, {"id": "I1", "t": "Football", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "Football is very popular in Cambodia. After school, boys and girls play on dusty fields or in the street, using a ball made of plastic or old cloth. On weekends, many people watch matches on television with their friends and shout when their team scores. Some children dream of becoming professional players, and they practise every day. Football teaches them to work together and to never give up.", "words": [["professional", "អាជីព"], ["score", "ស៊ុតចូល"], ["practise", "ហាត់សម"]]}, {"id": "I2", "t": "Volleyball", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "Volleyball is one of the favourite sports in Cambodian villages. In the evening, when the heat goes down, neighbours put up a net between two poles and divide into teams. Everyone, young and old, can join the game. People cheer, laugh and tease each other, and the match often continues until it is too dark to see the ball. It is a simple sport, but it brings the community together.", "words": [["net", "សំណាញ់"], ["tease", "ចំអកលេង"], ["divide", "ចែក"]]}, {"id": "I3", "t": "Pradal Serey", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "Pradal Serey is the Khmer form of kickboxing, and it is one of the country's most exciting sports. Fighters use their fists, elbows, knees and shins, and each match begins with a slow, traditional dance to show respect. Music plays throughout the fight, and the rhythm becomes faster as the action increases. Matches are shown on television, and the best fighters become famous across the country.", "words": [["fist", "កណ្តាប់ដៃ"], ["elbow", "កែងដៃ"], ["shin", "ស្មុង"]]}, {"id": "I4", "t": "Hosting the Games", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "In 2023, Cambodia hosted the Southeast Asian Games for the first time, and thousands of athletes came to Phnom Penh to compete. The government built new stadiums and improved roads, and volunteers helped visitors find their way. For many Cambodians, the event was a source of pride, because it showed that their country could organise a large international competition. It also inspired young people to try new sports.", "words": [["athlete", "កីឡាករ"], ["volunteer", "អ្នកស្ម័គ្រចិត្ត"], ["compete", "ប្រកួតប្រជែង"]]}, {"id": "I5", "t": "Learning to Swim", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "In a country with so many rivers and lakes, it is important to know how to swim. Many children first learn in a pond or a river with the help of an older brother or sister. Later, some schools teach swimming safely in a pool. After you learn, you feel confident near the water, and you can enjoy the water on a hot day. Remember: never swim alone.", "words": [["pond", "ស្រះ"], ["confident", "ជឿជាក់"], ["safely", "ដោយសុវត្ថិភាព"]]}, {"id": "I6", "t": "Khmer Chess", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "Ouk Chaktrang is the traditional Khmer version of chess, and it is older than the version played in the West. The pieces have names such as king, queen, elephant, horse and boat, and each moves in a special way. Players sit on mats under the shade of a tree and think for a long time before every move. Although the game is less common among young people today, clubs are working to teach it to the next generation.", "words": [["version", "កំណែ"], ["piece", "កូនអុក"], ["move", "ការដើរ"]]}, {"id": "I7", "t": "Photography", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "With a smartphone in every pocket, photography has become a popular hobby. Young people walk through markets, temples and rice fields to capture the colours and faces of their country. A good photo needs more than a good camera: it needs patience, good light and a clear idea of what you want to show. Many photographers share their pictures online, and some have started to earn money by selling prints or teaching others.", "words": [["capture", "ចាប់យក"], ["patience", "ការអត់ធ្មត់"], ["print", "រូបភាពបោះពុម្ព"]]}, {"id": "I8", "t": "Cycling in the Countryside", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Sports and Hobbies", "body": "On Sunday, we rent bicycles and ride along the quiet roads between the rice fields. The air is clean, and we pass small houses, water buffaloes and children waving from the roadside. Every few kilometres, we stop under a palm tree to drink water and rest. By the end of the day, our legs are tired, but our minds are free, and we promise to do it again.", "words": [["rent", "ជួល"], ["roadside", "ចិញ្ចើមផ្លូវ"], ["promise", "សន្យា"]]}, {"id": "J1", "t": "Elephants", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "The elephant is the largest land animal, and it has a special place in Khmer culture. You can see elephants carved on the walls of Angkor, where they carry kings and fight in wars. Elephants eat plants, drink a lot of water and live in family groups led by an older female. Today, wild elephants are in danger because their forests are smaller. People work to protect them and to stop illegal hunting.", "words": [["carve", "ឆ្លាក់"], ["hunting", "ការបរបាញ់"], ["female", "ញី"]]}, {"id": "J2", "t": "The Water Buffalo", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "In many villages, the water buffalo is a farmer's best friend. It is strong, calm and able to walk through deep mud, so it pulls the plough in the rice fields. After work, it likes to rest in a pond to stay cool. Children sometimes ride on its back, and they know every animal by name. Although tractors are becoming common, many families still keep buffaloes.", "words": [["plough", "នង្គ័ល"], ["mud", "ភក់"], ["tractor", "ត្រាក់ទ័រ"]]}, {"id": "J3", "t": "Monkeys at the Temple", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "Visitors to some Cambodian temples are surprised to meet groups of monkeys that live nearby. They sit on walls and stones, watching people closely. Some are shy, but others are bold and will grab food from a bag. It is better not to feed them or touch them, because they can bite and carry diseases. If you stay calm and keep your things closed, you can watch them safely.", "words": [["bold", "ក្លាហាន"], ["grab", "ចាប់ស្ទាក់"], ["disease", "ជំងឺ"]]}, {"id": "J4", "t": "The Mekong", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "The Mekong is one of the longest rivers in Asia, and it passes through six countries before it reaches the sea in southern Vietnam. In Cambodia, it brings rich soil to the fields and carries fish for millions of people. Boats move goods and passengers along it, and bridges and ferries connect the two banks. Because so many people depend on it, the health of the river is important for the whole region.", "words": [["region", "តំបន់"], ["ferry", "ចម្លង"], ["passenger", "អ្នកដំណើរ"]]}, {"id": "J5", "t": "Mangrove Forests", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "Along the coast near Koh Kong, mangrove trees grow with their roots in salty water. These roots protect the shore from waves, trap soil and give shelter to young fish and crabs, which is why mangroves are called the nurseries of the sea. They also store large amounts of carbon, helping to slow climate change. Unfortunately, many mangroves have been cut down for farms and shrimp ponds, but local communities are now replanting them.", "words": [["mangrove", "ព្រៃកោងកាង"], ["shelter", "ទីជម្រក"], ["replant", "ដាំឡើងវិញ"]]}, {"id": "J6", "t": "Bats at Sunset", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "Near Battambang, at the end of the day, millions of bats leave a cave and fly out in a long, dark line across the sky. Visitors gather in the fields to watch, and the sight lasts for more than an hour. The bats fly out to hunt insects and to find fruit, and in doing so, they help farmers by controlling pests. It is a natural spectacle that reminds us how much we depend on small creatures.", "words": [["cave", "ភ្នំរូង"], ["insect", "សត្វល្អិត"], ["spectacle", "ទិដ្ឋភាពអស្ចារ្យ"]]}, {"id": "J7", "t": "The Lotus", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "The lotus is a beautiful flower that grows in ponds and lakes. Its roots are in the mud, but its petals are clean and pink or white. In Khmer culture, the lotus is a sign of purity, and people offer it to Buddha at the pagoda. You can also eat its seeds and roots, and its big leaves are sometimes used to wrap food. Whenever I see a lotus, I feel peaceful.", "words": [["petal", "ស្លឹកផ្កា "], ["purity", "ភាពបរិសុទ្ធ"], ["wrap", "រុំ"]]}, {"id": "J8", "t": "Fireflies", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Animals and Nature", "body": "After dark, along some rivers in the south, the trees begin to shine with thousands of small lights. They are fireflies, and they flash together as if they were part of a magic show. The insects use the light to find a partner, and the glowing is strongest when the night is dark and the air is warm. A quiet boat trip is the best way to see them without frightening them.", "words": [["firefly", "ដង្កូវភ្លើង"], ["flash", "បញ្ចេញពន្លឺ"], ["frighten", "ធ្វើឲ្យភ័យ"]]}, {"id": "K1", "t": "Num Banh Chok", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "Num banh chok is a Khmer noodle dish that many people eat for breakfast. The noodles are made from fermented rice and are served with a green fish soup and many fresh herbs and vegetables, such as banana flower, cucumber and beans. You put the vegetables on top and mix everything with your spoon. The taste is light, sour and a little sweet. In the morning, women sell it from baskets at the market.", "words": [["noodles", "មី"], ["fermented", "ទឹកត្រី"], ["basket", "ល្អី"]]}, {"id": "K2", "t": "Prahok", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "Prahok is a salty paste made from fermented fish, and it is one of the most important flavours in Cambodian cooking. People add it to soups, dips and stir-fries, and its strong smell can surprise visitors. Making prahok takes several weeks, and families often prepare it after the fishing season. Although some foreigners find it unusual, many Cambodians say that a meal without prahok is not complete.", "words": [["paste", "ម្សៅលាយ"], ["flavour", "រសជាតិ"], ["complete", "ពេញលេញ"]]}, {"id": "K3", "t": "Palm Sugar", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "In the Cambodian countryside, tall palm trees stand in the middle of the fields. Men climb them every morning to collect the sweet juice from the flowers. The juice is then boiled in a large pan until it becomes thick, brown sugar that people use in desserts, soups and sauces. The work is difficult and requires strong legs and good balance, but it provides an income for many families.", "words": [["juice", "ទឹក"], ["boil", "ស្ងោរ"], ["balance", "តុល្យភាព"]]}, {"id": "K4", "t": "Sugarcane Juice", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "When the weather is hot, a glass of sugarcane juice is the best drink. The seller puts the long green sticks into a machine, which presses them and releases a sweet, cold juice. He adds a little ice and sometimes a drop of lime. It costs very little, and it gives you energy straight away. Children love it, and so do tired workers on their way home.", "words": [["sugarcane", "អំពៅ"], ["press", "សង្កត់/ច្របាច់"], ["lime", "ក្រូចឆ្មា "]]}, {"id": "K5", "t": "Coffee Culture", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "In recent years, coffee shops have become popular meeting places for young people in Cambodian cities. Students bring their laptops and study for hours, friends talk about their plans, and workers hold meetings with clients. Alongside Western-style drinks, many customers still like traditional iced coffee with sweet condensed milk. These shops offer free internet and a calm atmosphere, which is why they are often busy from morning until night.", "words": [["laptop", "កុំព្យូទ័រយួរដៃ"], ["client", "អតិថិជន"], ["condensed milk", "ទឹកដោះគោខាប់"]]}, {"id": "K6", "t": "Tropical Fruits", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "Cambodia has delicious fruit throughout the year. In the hot season, mangoes are sweet and juicy; later, you can taste rambutan, mangosteen and jackfruit. The durian, which has a strong smell, is called the king of fruits by its lovers. At the market, sellers cut the fruit into small pieces and put them in a bag with a stick. Fruit is a healthy snack, and it is cheap when it is in season.", "words": [["juicy", "មានទឹកច្រើន"], ["in season", "ក្នុងរដូវ"], ["jackfruit", "ខ្នុរ"]]}, {"id": "K7", "t": "The Sampeah", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Food and Customs", "body": "In Cambodia, people greet each other not with a handshake but with the sampeah. You press your palms together in front of your chest, lower your head slightly and say hello. The higher your hands are, the more respect you show: when meeting a teacher or an older person, your thumbs touch your chin, and for a monk, they touch your nose. Foreigners who use the sampeah are often warmly appreciated.", "words": [["greet", "ជំរាបសួរ"], ["palm", "បាតដៃ"], ["appreciate", "ឲ្យតម្លៃ"]]}, {"id": "L1", "t": "When I Feel Nervous", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "Before an exam or a speech, I often feel nervous. My hands are cold, my heart beats fast, and I forget my words. When this happens, I take a deep breath and count to ten slowly. I also tell myself, \"I am ready, and I can do it.\" After a few minutes, I feel calmer. Everyone gets nervous sometimes, so it is not a problem.", "words": [["nervous", "ភ័យ"], ["breath", "ដង្ហើម"], ["calm", "ស្ងប់"]]}, {"id": "L2", "t": "My Dream", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "Since I was a child, I have dreamed of becoming a teacher. I like to explain things, and I enjoy seeing a student understand something new. To achieve this, I study hard and I read books every night. It will not be easy, because the course is long and expensive, but I have a plan. I believe that if you work every day, your dream can become reality.", "words": [["achieve", "សម្រេចបាន"], ["reality", "ការពិត"], ["explain", "ពន្យល់"]]}, {"id": "L3", "t": "A Letter to Myself", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "If I could write to myself ten years from now, I would say: be proud of how far you have come. Remember the nights you were tired but kept studying, and the people who helped you without asking for anything. Do not be afraid to change your plans, because life rarely follows a straight line. Above all, stay curious and kind. These two qualities will protect you better than any diploma.", "words": [["diploma", "សញ្ញាបត្រ"], ["curious", "ចង់ដឹងចង់ឃើញ"], ["rarely", "កម្រ"]]}, {"id": "L4", "t": "Learning from Failure", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "Nobody likes to fail, but failure is often the best teacher. When an experiment goes wrong, a scientist asks what happened and tries again with a new idea. When a business closes, the owner learns which mistakes to avoid next time. The difficulty is that failure hurts our pride, so we hide it. People who share their mistakes openly, however, help others to avoid them and often become more confident themselves.", "words": [["experiment", "ការពិសោធន៍"], ["pride", "មោទនភាព"], ["openly", "ដោយបើកចំហ"]]}, {"id": "L5", "t": "Small Acts of Kindness", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "Kindness does not need money. You can smile at a stranger, carry a bag for an old woman or share your umbrella in the rain. These actions take only a minute, but the person remembers them for a long time. When you are kind, you also feel happier. If everyone did one kind thing every day, the world would be a better place.", "words": [["stranger", "មនុស្សដែលមិនស្គាល់"], ["umbrella", "ឆត្រ"], ["kind", "ចិត្តល្អ"]]}, {"id": "L6", "t": "The Value of Patience", "lv": "B1", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "Learning anything new takes time, and patience is the secret that many people forget. A tree does not grow in one day, and a language is not learned in a week. When progress feels slow, remember that you are improving even if you cannot see it. Keep your daily habit, celebrate small steps and be gentle with yourself. One year from now, you will be happy that you did not stop.", "words": [["secret", "អាថ៌កំបាំង"], ["celebrate", "អបអរសាទរ"], ["gentle", "ទន់ភ្លន់"]]}, {"id": "L7", "t": "What Home Means", "lv": "B2", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "Home is not only a building; it is a feeling. For some people, it is the smell of rice cooking at sunrise; for others, it is the sound of a mother's voice calling them for dinner. When you leave for study or work in another city or country, you begin to understand how much these small details matter. Wherever you go, you carry your home with you in your language, your food and your memories.", "words": [["detail", "ព័ត៌មានលម្អិត"], ["memory", "ការចងចាំ"], ["carry", "កាន់/ដឹក"]]}, {"id": "L8", "t": "My Mother's Cooking", "lv": "A2", "grp": "Feelings and Life", "body": "My mother cooks every evening after work. She is not a professional, but her food is the best in the world. The kitchen smells of garlic, lemongrass and fresh herbs, and I always try to taste a little before dinner. She smiles and says, \"Wait, it is not ready.\" When we finally sit down together, the first bite makes me feel at home. I hope that one day I can cook for her.", "words": [["garlic", "ខ្ទឹមស"], ["lemongrass", "ស្លឹកគ្រៃ"], ["bite", "ម៉ាត់"]]}], "prompts": [["Describe your morning routine.", "A2"], ["What is your favourite Cambodian food? Explain why.", "A2"], ["Write about a festival in your family.", "B1"], ["How do you travel to work or school?", "B1"], ["Explain what you know about the Moon or the Sun.", "B1"], ["Do smartphones make our lives better or worse?", "B2"], ["What can ordinary people do to protect the environment?", "B2"]], "secs": [["A", "កម្ពុជា (Cambodia)"], ["B", "ជីវិតប្រចាំថ្ងៃ (Daily Life)"], ["C", "សកលលោក និងពិភពលោក (The Universe)"], ["D", "កម្រិត C1–C2 (Advanced Reading)"]], "title": "Reading Book: Cambodia, Daily Life and the Universe", "howto": ["អានលើកទី ១ ដោយមិនមើលពាក្យ ហើយទាយពីបរិបទ គោលដៅគឺយល់គំនិតសំខាន់។", "គូសបន្ទាត់ក្រោមពាក្យថ្មី រួចមើលបញ្ជី Words ខាងក្រោម។", "អានលើកទី ២ យឺតៗ ហើយអានឮៗ (អានឮបង្កើនការនិយាយ និងការបញ្ចេញសំឡេង)។", "ឆ្លើយសំណួរដោយមិនមើលអត្ថបទ រួចមើលចម្លើយ។", "និយាយឡើងវិញ (retell) ដោយពាក្យខ្លួនឯង ៣–៥ ប្រយោគ ឬសរសេរសង្ខេប។"]};
